---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

// Parked 2026-09-14 (research/site-audit-2026-09-14.md) and rewritten the same day so that
// restoring it is a rename. Where a resource has been used, say what it turned up and when,
// negatives included, rather than how to search it. Name no candidate unit and no person
// whose identity is an open question to Larry; link no parked section but research/sources.
---

<BaseLayout title="Online resources">
  <span class="eyebrow">Research toolkit</span>
  <h1>Online resources</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Public resources on the web that can move the <a href={withBase('/research/')}>open
    questions</a> forward, grouped by cost, with what each has turned up so far. The two most
    decisive record sets — Larry's personnel file and unit morning reports — are <em>not</em>
    online anywhere; those come only by written request to the National Personnel Records
    Center (see the end of this page).
  </p>

  <h2>Free</h2>
  <ul class="resource-list">
    <li>
      <p class="record">Digital archive &middot; Texas Tech University</p>
      <h3><a href="https://vva.vietnam.ttu.edu">Virtual Vietnam Archive</a></h3>
      <p>
        Over 672,000 digitized items &mdash; some four million pages &mdash; free to search and
        download: unit records, after-action reports, period military maps, photographs, and
        oral histories. The richest single place to look for period material on T&acirc;y Ninh
        and N&uacute;i B&agrave; &#272;en in 1964&ndash;65. Several of the documents cited on
        <a href={withBase('/sources/')}>Sources</a> came from it in September 2026: the Special
        Forces camp lists and order of battle, the provincial reports for July to September 1965,
        and the index of positions below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three things the archive's own reference staff told us in September 2026, which are not
        obvious from the search box. Its holdings come <em>only</em> by donation, so it is not
        where government record series live &mdash; those are at the National Archives, below.
        Only about a fifth of what it holds is digitized; the rest is findable solely through
        each collection's Finding Aid, and staff will scan a named box and folder on request.
        And there is a second door, the
        <a href="https://readingroom.vietnam.ttu.edu/">Vietnam Reading Room</a> &mdash; the same
        search, but a free login there opens digitized items the public portal keeps closed for
        copyright.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Period maps &middot; Texas Tech &middot; free downloads</p>
      <h3><a href="https://vva.vietnam.ttu.edu/">Vietnam Archive map search</a></h3>
      <p>
        Behind the <em>Maps</em> tab of the archive above, a search form built for cartography
        &mdash; latitude and longitude, grid zone, scale, sheet number &mdash; over the U.S.
        Army&rsquo;s wartime 1:50,000 topographic sheets of Vietnam, downloadable as
        high-resolution PDFs, with a companion 1:25,000 pictorial series that staff will email on
        request by sheet number. These are the maps the men on the ground actually used. The
        sheet covering N&uacute;i B&agrave; &#272;en and T&acirc;y Ninh was downloaded on 1
        September 2026; the copy online is a 1970 revision, so it shows the ground five years
        after Larry&rsquo;s tour, not during it. A veteran-compiled
        <a href="https://www.vietnam.ttu.edu/virtualarchive/items.php?item=25720206001">index of
        fire support bases and landing zones</a> converts many wartime place names into sheet
        numbers and coordinates.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Casualty memorial &middot; unit-indexed</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.virtualwall.org/">The Virtual Wall</a></h3>
      <p>
        Memorial pages for every name on the Vietnam Veterans Memorial, browsable by unit of
        assignment (unit data derived from the volunteer-researched Coffelt Database). The names
        behind it come from the national casualty file, which this project searched directly
        (see the National Archives, below), so the Wall adds context and remembrance rather than
        new names.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Casualty memorial &middot; name search &middot; outreach</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.vvmf.org/Wall-of-Faces/">Wall of Faces</a></h3>
      <p>
        The Vietnam Veterans Memorial Fund's photo-and-remembrance page for each of the 58,000+
        names. Posted remembrances sometimes reach family members and unit-mates &mdash; a quiet
        outreach channel.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Veterans association &middot; outreach</p>
      <h3><a href="https://tsna.org/">Tan Son Nhut Association</a></h3>
      <p>
        Association of veterans who served at T&acirc;n S&#417;n Nh&#7913;t &mdash; predominantly
        Air Force, though the base hosted Army personnel too &mdash; with guestbooks and bulletin
        boards. If &ldquo;Ton Son Nuht,&rdquo; as Larry spells it, is T&acirc;n S&#417;n
        Nh&#7913;t &mdash; the leading hypothesis &mdash; this is a way to test it and a channel
        for finding men who were there in 1964&ndash;65.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Official histories &middot; free PDFs</p>
      <h3><a href="https://history.army.mil/">U.S. Army Center of Military History</a></h3>
      <p>
        Free official histories of the war, including the airmobility volumes that trace the
        Army aviation build-up in 1964&ndash;65 and, with the Army Medical Department, the
        medical volumes that document the hospitals in Japan. The Air Force and Marine Corps
        history offices likewise publish free PDFs of the volumes cited on the
        <a href={withBase('/sources/')}>Sources</a> page.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Government archive catalog &middot; casualty file</p>
      <h3><a href="https://catalog.archives.gov/">National Archives Catalog</a></h3>
      <p>
        Finding aids and a growing body of digitized series, including Record Group 472
        (Records of the U.S. Forces in Southeast Asia, 1950&ndash;1975 — MACV and all services).
        The place to scope what exists before requesting unit records; a request in that record
        group has been drafted and not yet sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        The National Archives also publishes the national file of American fatal casualties of
        the war as a free download. Searched in September 2026: <strong>no Hunnewell, in any
        spelling, anywhere among the American dead</strong>, and no Lowry, Lowery or Lowrey who
        died in 1964&ndash;65 in a way that could be the sergeant Larry remembers. The same file
        was used to check the <em>Stars and Stripes</em> casualty lists of autumn 1965 name by
        name.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Veterans association &middot; partially public</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.vhpa.org/">Vietnam Helicopter Pilots Association</a></h3>
      <p>
        A pilots' association spanning the Vietnam era, 1961&ndash;75, whose public value here
        is its photo collection and helicopter-incident pages; the member directory itself is
        member-oriented. Context for Larry's helicopter-travel recollections and another
        outreach avenue.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Search by name</h2>
  <p>
    Sites built to be queried by a person's name — for Larry himself, for the men he names, and
    for the families and unit-mates who might still be reached. The casualty pages above are
    name-searchable too; these add the living, the buried, and the printed record.
  </p>
  <ul class="resource-list">
    <li>
      <p class="record">Genealogy database &middot; free (free account)</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.familysearch.org/search/">FamilySearch</a></h3>
      <p>
        The largest free genealogy service, run by the LDS Church. Name-searchable across
        censuses, the Social Security Death Index, obituaries, and U.S. military collections —
        the free counterpart to <a href="https://www.ancestry.com/">Ancestry</a> below. Its
        Korean/Vietnam Military Index lists the war dead by name; some record sets are viewable
        only from a FamilySearch Center or affiliate library.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Cemetery database &middot; free, no account</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/search">Find a Grave</a></h3>
      <p>
        Over 230 million memorials, searchable by name with no login. A memorial typically
        carries the birth and death dates, a headstone photo, and the obituary text. In August
        2026 it was the first record to connect the name Larry remembers as &ldquo;Ron
        Tototz&rdquo; to the spelling his family used, Totosz. A miss is information too: no
        grave for a man Larry served with points away from a wartime death.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Government burial database &middot; free</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.cem.va.gov/nationwide-gravesite-locator/">VA Nationwide Gravesite Locator</a></h3>
      <p>
        The National Cemetery Administration's search of veterans buried in VA national, state,
        and military cemeteries — plus private graves bearing a government marker. Search by name,
        including alternate spellings. A hit confirms a death and points to the cemetery; a miss
        says only that the man is not in a VA-marked grave.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Veteran registry &middot; free to join &middot; outreach</p>
      <h3><a href="https://togetherweserved.com/">Together We Served</a></h3>
      <p>
        A veteran-built directory of 2.5&nbsp;million-plus members across all branches, WWII to
        present, where veterans record their service history by unit and dates. Free to join;
        free members can look up and contact five people a month. A live channel for reaching
        anyone who served alongside Larry. (<a href="https://www.vetfriends.com/">VetFriends</a>
        is a similar registry, but searching it requires a paid membership.)
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Digitized books &middot; free full-text search</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.hathitrust.org/">HathiTrust</a></h3>
      <p>
        Full-text search across millions of digitized books and government documents. Public-domain
        volumes are fully readable; in-copyright ones are searchable for a name even when the page
        image is withheld. The place to catch a soldier named in a unit yearbook, a
        &ldquo;welcome aboard&rdquo; booklet, or a published unit history — complementing the
        official histories from the <a href="https://history.army.mil/">Center of Military History</a>
        above.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Historic newspapers &middot; free &middot; through 1963</p>
      <h3><a href="https://chroniclingamerica.loc.gov/">Chronicling America</a></h3>
      <p>
        The Library of Congress's free, name-searchable archive of digitized American newspapers.
        Coverage runs only <em>through 1963</em>, so it cannot hold the 1965 killed-in-action
        report. Its use here is earlier and local: hometown items and family mentions. Its
        companion Directory of U.S. Newspapers (1690&ndash;present) helps identify which hometown
        paper holds what, and where its microfilm is kept.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Paid</h2>
  <ul class="resource-list">
    <li>
      <p class="record">Newspaper archive &middot; day pass $4.95, monthly $7.95 (rates as of 2026)</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.stripes.com/help/archives-faq/">Stars and Stripes historic archives</a></h3>
      <p>
        Every Pacific edition 1948&ndash;1999, digitized and keyword-searchable via
        NewspaperArchive. Used from August to September 2026 for the killed-in-action report.
        <strong>What it turned up: nothing naming Larry.</strong> The name Hunnewell and eleven
        ways a scan can garble it return no hits in 1964&ndash;66, and every Vietnam casualty list
        the Pacific edition printed from September to December 1965 was found and read by eye;
        none names him. Keyword search alone could not have settled this: casualty lists are
        columns of small type that text recognition garbles, which is why the lists were read
        page by page.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Newspaper archive &middot; subscription</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.newspapers.com/">Newspapers.com</a></h3>
      <p>
        A large archive of American local papers. Searched on a trial in August 2026: no item
        about Larry in any paper it holds for 1962&ndash;66. It does not carry Pacific <em>Stars
        and Stripes</em>, and it does not carry the Inglewood hometown paper, whose 1965
        issues have not been found in digitised form or yet searched on microfilm.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Genealogy subscription</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/1139">Ancestry</a></h3>
      <p>
        Holds <em>Stars and Stripes</em> Pacific editions only through 1963 — too early for the
        KIA report — plus general military collections. Worth a search mainly if the family
        already has a subscription.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Military records subscription</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.fold3.com/">Fold3</a></h3>
      <p>
        Ancestry's military-records site, digitizing selected National Archives series.
        Vietnam-era coverage is thinner than for the World Wars; check the free index for
        relevant hits before paying.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not online</h2>
  <p>
    Larry's service file and his hospital records from Japan exist only at the National
    Personnel Records Center, and come only by a request the veteran signs. The family's own
    copy of his DD-214 turned up in August 2026; it names his last assignment, in Japan, but not
    his unit in Vietnam. His signed request (Standard Form 180) was mailed on 17 August 2026, and
    the certified-mail receipt came back in September; no reply has arrived yet, and none is
    expected soon. Company morning reports &mdash; the top of this project's
    <a href={withBase('/research/')}>source priorities</a>, naming each man day by day &mdash;
    are on microfilm at the same records center and can be asked for once his file names his
    unit.
  </p>

  <p><a href={withBase('/research/')}>&larr; Research</a></p>
</BaseLayout>

